    <body>
      <Value><![CDATA[## The Problem: A Navigation That Stays in Sync

When you're building a blogging site in a rush, it's tempting to hardcode navigation into every template. Copy-paste the menu here, paste it there, and suddenly you've got six versions of "about" floating around with three different URLs. Spoiler alert: that's how broken links happen, and broken links during a live demo are my personal nightmare.

I decided early on that navigation had to live in exactly one place. That's not just good practice—it's survival.

## The Solution: A Razor Partial to Rule Them All

Umbraco's partial views became my secret weapon. I created a single `Navigation.cshtml` partial that queries the content tree, builds the menu dynamically, and gets included in every page template. No duplication, no manual updates when we add a new blog post—just one source of truth.

The partial walks the Umbraco document hierarchy, checks published status, and renders only what should actually be visible. It's respectfully simple, using `Model.Root()` and iterating visible children.

This approach means the nav stays automatically in sync with your content structure. Publish a new blog list page? It appears in the navigation instantly. Archive an old page? It disappears just as fast.

## Why This Matters for UX

Consistent navigation isn't just about not breaking things—it's about trust. When a user can predict where the menu is, what it says, and where it leads, the site feels intentional and polished. During a live demo, that consistency is everything. The audience isn't thinking about Razor partials; they're thinking "this feels like a real website."

Having one partial also meant I could style navigation consistently across mobile and desktop with a single CSS rule set. No fighting with different markup structures or accidentally hiding the menu on a different page.

## The Zero Broken Links Promise

Because the navigation pulls URLs directly from Umbraco's published content, there's no way to hardcode a wrong URL. The system builds links from the source of truth, so every navigation item that renders is guaranteed to point somewhere real. That's the zero broken links promise—not through heroic testing, but through architecture that makes broken links impossible.

## What I Learned

The best technical decisions are often the ones that make future decisions automatic. By centralizing navigation in a partial, I eliminated the category of errors that come from duplication. That's a pattern I'm carrying forward—find the single source of truth, and let it do the work.]]></Value>
    </body>
    <excerpt>
      <Value><![CDATA[Building a shared Razor partial for navigation meant one source of truth, automatic sync with content, and zero broken links during the demo.]]></Value>
